PATH / MODULE RECONCILIATION (no silent drift — covenant #9)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Gradle module: the prompt gate says `:app:` -> the real module is `:android`.
   Evidence: apps/android/settings.gradle.kts L33-36
     include(":android"); project(":android").projectDir = file("app")
     ("every P3/A* prompt gate addresses `:android:`").
   `:app:assembleRelease` fails with "project 'app' not found"; `:android:` is the
   real, gate-compliant task name and is used below.

2. Source path: KEPT at the prompt's mandated, allowed-files location
     apps/android/app/src/main/java/com/teslasync/widget-primitives/WidgetBigNumber/
   (this is also the gate's check-placeholders -Path, so the scan runs over the
   real files as written). The directory cannot back a valid Kotlin package
   (hyphen + PascalCase leaf), so the package intentionally diverges to
     io.teslasync.android.widgetprimitives.widgetbignumber
   with @file:Suppress("InvalidPackageDeclaration"), exactly as the sibling
   presentational surfaces do (com/teslasync/shared-surfaces/StaggerContainer.kt,
   com/teslasync/shared-surfaces/AnimatedNumber/AnimatedNumber.kt) and the sibling
   dashboard surface (com/teslasync/dashboard-widgets/RangeBarWidget/RangeBarWidget.kt).
   This passes ktlint + detekt (LINT_EXIT=0 below, warningsAsErrors=true), so the
   templated path is NOT rejected by the repo's quality gate for this artifact —
   the suppress is the sanctioned mechanism and the mandated path is preserved.
   The co-located tests use the real package path (no suppress needed):
     src/test/java/io/teslasync/android/widgetprimitives/widgetbignumber/
     src/androidTest/java/io/teslasync/android/widgetprimitives/widgetbignumber/

3. Placeholder false positive opt-out: the shared formatter call
   `animatable.value.toDouble()` trips the scanner's case-insensitive "TODO"
   substring match (it contains "toDo"). It carries a `// parity:allow ... not a
   TODO` marker (PLACEHOLDER_ALLOWED=1, not a violation), identical to the sibling
   AnimatedNumber surface's handling of the same `.toDouble()` call.

